  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 252 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nRidging will continue today across central Indiana, but a shortwave \nwill develop on the periphery and push just to the north and east. A \nweak outflow boundary is currently draped across NE IL and NW IN, \nbut latest observations are showing this boundary becoming more \ndiffuse as it progresses eastward. This trend is expected to \ncontinue with minimal impacts over central Indiana outside of a \nslight increase in diurnal cumulus. \n\nMost CAMs depict additional convective development later this \nafternoon over northern IN where the shortwave is located, with a \ngeneral progression E/NE during the evening. While parameter spacing \ndoes not support convective development over central Indiana, cold \npool development may allow for decaying thunderstorms or light \nshowers to reach northern central Indiana between 7-10PM. \nTemperatures and heat index values will be high once again today. \nThere is some signs of mixing this afternoon which may drop dew \npoints a few degrees. A few occasional gusts to 18kt will also be \npossible as the PBL becomes mixed this afternoon.\n\nConditions are expected to be very similar tomorrow, with a near \nidentical synoptic setup. The main difference is the position of a \nmid level boundary and additional moisture convergence through the \n850-700mb layer. This may allow for enough saturation in this layer \nfor isolated convective showers and thunderstorms in the late \nafternoon and evening. While severe weather is not expected, DCAPE \nof 900-1000 J/KG may support a few downbursts within this \"popcorn\" \nconvection to approach severe criteria. Where this mid level \nboundary situates tomorrow will also be the primary location for \nadditional MCS development tomorrow afternoon through Saturday \nmorning. This is currently expected to remain NE of central Indiana, \nbut the area of concern will be close enough to where a brief \nglancing from organized thunderstorms will be possible over far NE \nportions of the area. \n\nThe mid level boundary will have more synoptic support Saturday, \nwith continental polar air attempting to move into the area from the \nNE late Saturday into Saturday night. This should generally increase \nconvective activity across the area though moisture convergence and \nincreased wind shear. Confidence on where greatest thunderstorm \ncoverage will be located is low at this time. \n\nA brief cooldown is likely Sunday through Monday as a deepening \ntrough over the East Coast, pushes the ridge westward. High pressure \nis expected to build beneath upper level confluence leading to a \ndrying trend with highs in the mid 80s with dew points in the upper \n50s to mid 60s. Towards the middle of next week, the ridge is \nexpected to build back over the region, with an additional stretch \nof well above normal temperatures and humidity.",
